module dft/pwdft
  The dense engine: at every k-point, build the Kohn-Sham Hamiltonian H(k)
  as a matrix in the plane-wave basis and diagonalize it.

  This is the direct way to solve the problem, and the reason it exists is
  to be the check on the iterative engine (dft/pwdft_it.viv): the two share
  dft/pw_common.viv and nothing else of the solver. It runs silicon and
  aluminum; the other materials have too many plane waves for it.

  H(k) in the plane-wave basis has three parts:
  - kinetic energy, diagonal: `|k + G|^2 / 2`;
  - the local potential, which depends only on the difference of the two
    plane waves: `V_tot(G - G')`, read off the FFT grid;
  - the nonlocal pseudopotential `V_nl(G, G')`, built once per k-point.

  The file runs after the deck, dft/kmesh.viv and dft/pw_common.viv. Each
  SCF k-point gets a slot in the arrays below, and one more slot is scratch
  space for the points of the band path. The top level then runs the steps
  listed under `<main>` when you view this lens: bases, the SCF loop, the
  bands along the path, and their zero of energy. The loop starts from a
  uniform density and stops when both the density change and the energy
  change fall below SCF_TOL. For silicon it needs the same number of
  iterations as the numpy reference, which does the same arithmetic in a
  different language [test "SCF converged, in the same number of iterations as the reference"].

setup_slot  @f220a1d7
  what  Fill one slot for a k-point given in fractional coordinates: the
        integer triple m of every plane wave that passes the cutoff, its
        `k + G` in Cartesian coordinates and its length, every radial
        projector at that length, and the structure-factor phase of every
        atom. Returns the largest `|m_i|` used.
  why   Everything here depends on k alone, so it is computed once per
        k-point and reused at every SCF iteration.

  for each i1
    for each i2
      for each i3
        if the plane wave passes the cutoff
          keep        record m, `k + G` and `|k + G|`, and track the largest `|m_i|`
          projectors  evaluate each radial projector `p_i^l(|k + G|)`
          phases      the structure factor `exp(-i G.tau)` of each atom, from G alone, not `k + G`

vnl_element  @604fc4d7
  what  One element `V_nl(G_i, G_j)` of the nonlocal pseudopotential of a
        slot: the pair of plane waves i and j.
  how   The cosine of the angle between `k + G_i` and `k + G_j`; then for
        each species, the pair's structure factor (the sum over that
        species' atoms of `exp(-i (G_i - G_j).tau)`) times the radial part
        `sum_l (2l+1)/(4 pi) P_l(cos) sum_ab p_a h_ab p_b`, with the
        projectors p evaluated at `|k + G_i|` and `|k + G_j|`.
  why   The Legendre form needs no spherical harmonics. The iterative
        engine uses the equivalent separable form instead. Both engines are
        compared with the same numpy reference, so their agreement with it
        checks each form
        [test "total energy agrees with the numpy reference"].

build_vnl  @fd08fcb1
  what  The nonlocal pseudopotential of one slot as a matrix in its
        plane-wave basis, one vnl_element per pair.
  why   The matrix is Hermitian, so only the lower triangle is computed and
        each element's conjugate is written into the upper one.

build_vtot  @0fd3e8ea
  what  The total local potential `V_tot(G) = V_ps + V_H + V_xc` on the FFT
        grid, from the density and the exchange-correlation potential in G
        space. Returns the Hartree energy.
  how   Poisson's equation is algebraic in G space:
        `V_H(G) = 4 pi rho(G) / |G|^2`, with `G = 0` left out (see gth_vloc
        in dft/pw_common.intent). The Hartree energy is
        `(OMEGA / 2) sum_G 4 pi |rho(G)|^2 / |G|^2`.

build_h  @10494150
  what  The Hamiltonian H(k) of one slot as an `NPWMAX x NPWMAX` matrix:
        `V_tot(G - G') + V_nl(G, G')` everywhere, plus the kinetic energy on
        the diagonal.
  why   Every k-point has its own number of plane waves, but the eigensolver
        is handed one fixed-size matrix. Rows past the slot's own count are
        padding: they are decoupled from the rest and given diagonal values
        far above the physical spectrum, so they are never among the
        occupied bands.

add_density  @06c816a7
  what  Add the density of one k-point's occupied bands to acc and return
        the sum.
  how   For each band with nonzero occupation, place its coefficients on the
        FFT grid, inverse FFT to get `psi(r)`, and add
        `weight * occupation * |psi(r)|^2`, normalized by the cell volume.
